# The repository had no .gitignore at all, and the walkthrough tells you to create the CDK
# app inside this directory, so following it produces every one of the paths below with
# nothing stopping them from being committed.

# Synthesized cloud assemblies. cdk.out holds the generated CloudFormation templates and any
# bundled Lambda assets, so it can contain whatever your stack embeds.
cdk.out/
*.d.ts
!lambda/*.d.ts

# cdk.context.json is deliberately NOT ignored. AWS is explicit that it must be tracked:
# "Because they're part of your application's state, cdk.json and cdk.context.json must be
# committed to source control along with the rest of your app's source code. Otherwise,
# deployments in other environments (for example, a CI pipeline) might produce inconsistent
# results." It caches the answers to environment queries, which is what freezes an AMI id or
# an availability-zone list so synthesis stays deterministic.
#
# Worth knowing before you commit one: its KEYS embed the account id, for example
# availability-zones:account=123456789012:region=eu-central-1. So in a public repository,
# read the file before committing it. This project generates none, because it performs no
# context lookups.
# https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cdk/v2/guide/context.html

# Compiled output. A TypeScript CDK project emits .js and .d.ts beside every .ts source, so
# without this the repository fills with build artifacts that shadow the real sources.
*.js
!jest.config.js
*.js.map
!projects/*/jest.config.js

node_modules/

# Credentials and secrets.
#
# BOTH glob forms, because neither implies the other, and the earlier version of this file had
# only one of them. `.env*` is a prefix glob: it catches .env, .env.local, and .envrc, which
# direnv reads and which people put exported credentials in. `*.env` is a suffix glob, for
# names like audit.env or production.env. What used to be here was `.env` plus `.env.*`, and
# the dot is not cosmetic: `.env.*` requires a SECOND dot, so it never matched .envrc, and
# nothing at all matched audit.env.
#
# The negation is the other half. `.env*` also matches .env.example, the one file in this
# family you are meant to commit, so it is re-included explicitly. Order matters: a negation
# has to follow the pattern it undoes, since the last matching rule wins.
.env*
*.env
!.env.example
.aws/
credentials*
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx

# SSH private keys, which none of the patterns above cover: an SSH key in its default
# filename has no extension at all. `id_rsa*` alone is not enough either, because
# ssh-keygen's default type is ed25519 now rather than rsa (man ssh-keygen: "ed25519
# (the default)"), so id_ed25519 is the name a key generated today gets. This matters
# here more than in most of these repositories: the walkthrough has you SSH to EC2.
# Enumerated rather than globbed as `id_*`, which would also match ordinary sources.
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
id_ecdsa*
id_dsa*
*.ppk
.ssh/

# Test and coverage output.
coverage/
.nyc_output/
junit.xml

# Editor and OS noise.
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
*.log
# Both sqlite spellings: the extension is a convention rather than a rule, and .sqlite3 is at
# least as common as .sqlite.
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
__pycache__/
